The Mercedes-Benz C-Class C200 CDI refers to a diesel variant of the W204 generation, particularly the facelifted models produced from 2011 to 2014. This evaluation is based on the W204 C200 CDI facelift model relative to the current automotive market.

*   **price\_usd**: As a used car from the early 2010s, the price is significantly lower than a new car today. Used prices range from around €5,200 to €10,500 (approx $5,600 - $11,300 USD) (Source [18, 37]) or $9,000 - $13,900 AUD (approx $5,900 - $9,100 USD) (Source [29]). These prices are well below the $35,000 average for a new car. Score: 0.80.
*   **total\_cost\_estimate\_5yr\_usd**: While the purchase price is low, an older luxury car will have higher maintenance and potential repair costs than a new economy car (Source [1, 25, 26, 35]). Score: -0.20.
*   **insurance\_usd\_yr**: Insurance groups for the W204 C-Class C200 CDI were moderate for its time, typically in the mid-range (Group 26-29 in the UK) (Source [41, 42]). Relative to modern cars, this is likely around average or slightly below. Score: 0.00.
*   **maint\_usd\_yr**: Maintenance costs for a Mercedes-Benz are generally higher than average (Source [1, 25, 26, 35]). Estimated annual costs increase with age (Source [26]). Score: -0.50.
*   **repair\_risk\_score**: The W204 generation is generally considered reliable for a Mercedes of its era, especially facelifted models with the OM651 engine (Source [22, 23, 27, 32]). However, early OM651 engines had potential issues with piezo injectors (later reverted to solenoid type) and some reports of timing chain tensioner issues exist, though less common than on some other engines (Source [21, 23, 27]). Other potential issues include DPF clogging and suspension wear (Source [1, 8, 21]). Risk is higher than a top-tier reliable brand but better than some other luxury makes. CarEdge rates the C-Class as worse than average for major repair probability over 10 years (Source [26]). Score: -0.20.
*   **hp**: The C200 CDI (facelift) had 136 hp (Source [2, 3, 6, 10, 19, 44]). This is below the 200 hp average. Score: -0.50.
*   **torque**: The C200 CDI (facelift) had 360 Nm (265.52 lb-ft) (Source [3, 6, 10, 19]). This is well above the 190 lb-ft average. Score: 0.50.
